WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.142
Lesson two point eight.

2
00:00:01.432 --> 00:00:03.270
How a song on disk becomes a training

3
00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:03.560
batch.

4
00:00:07.483 --> 00:00:09.713
Lesson two point three put the notes of a

5
00:00:09.713 --> 00:00:10.510
song on a grid.

6
00:00:10.829 --> 00:00:12.953
This lesson follows a song from disk to

7
00:00:12.953 --> 00:00:13.484
the model.

8
00:00:13.802 --> 00:00:15.661
The store keeps every song's notes.

9
00:00:15.979 --> 00:00:18.316
The cache keeps a fast, flat copy of what

10
00:00:18.316 --> 00:00:19.112
training reads.

11
00:00:19.431 --> 00:00:21.555
And the loader turns songs into batches.

12
00:00:21.873 --> 00:00:23.785
The same three serve Lakh and XMIDI.

13
00:00:27.257 --> 00:00:29.389
The store holds one line of text per song.

14
00:00:29.694 --> 00:00:31.826
It keeps the file's ticks per beat, its

15
00:00:31.826 --> 00:00:33.857
tempo map and its time signatures:

16
00:00:33.857 --> 00:00:35.177
everything the grid needs.

17
00:00:35.482 --> 00:00:37.919
Then three lists of notes: guitar, drums

18
00:00:37.919 --> 00:00:38.376
and bass.

19
00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.813
Each note is a time in seconds, a pitch

20
00:00:40.813 --> 00:00:41.574
and a velocity.

21
00:00:41.879 --> 00:00:44.062
Only note starts are kept, not note ends.

22
00:00:44.463 --> 00:00:45.993
The lines are packed into sixteen

23
00:00:45.993 --> 00:00:47.974
compressed shards, by the first character

24
00:00:47.974 --> 00:00:48.784
of each song's id.

25
00:00:49.054 --> 00:00:50.809
Lakh v3 holds fifty-two thousand seven

26
00:00:50.809 --> 00:00:52.610
hundred and ninety-three songs, about

27
00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:54.050
ninety-seven million drum notes.

28
00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.256
XMIDI holds forty thousand six hundred and

29
00:00:56.256 --> 00:00:57.066
ninety-four songs.

30
00:00:57.336 --> 00:00:59.407
The windows aren't stored: they're built

31
00:00:59.407 --> 00:01:01.117
from these lists every time a song is

32
00:01:01.117 --> 00:01:03.053
read, with lesson two point three's grid.

33
00:01:06.517 --> 00:01:08.799
Reading one song from the store on its own

34
00:01:08.799 --> 00:01:11.293
takes about nine tenths of a second: open

35
00:01:11.293 --> 00:01:13.734
a shard, find the line, parse it, build

36
00:01:13.734 --> 00:01:14.371
the windows.

37
00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:16.282
About nine kilobytes come out.

38
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.723
So the loader doesn't read songs one by

39
00:01:18.723 --> 00:01:20.634
one: it sweeps each shard once.

40
00:01:20.952 --> 00:01:23.128
Even so, decompressing and parsing the

41
00:01:23.128 --> 00:01:25.357
forty-seven thousand training songs costs

42
00:01:25.357 --> 00:01:27.745
about four minutes every epoch, before any

43
00:01:27.745 --> 00:01:28.647
learning happens.

44
00:01:28.966 --> 00:01:31.354
Both figures are reported in the code, not

45
00:01:31.354 --> 00:01:32.256
re-measured here.

46
00:01:32.575 --> 00:01:34.698
So the cache does that work once, and

47
00:01:34.698 --> 00:01:35.918
writes the result flat.

48
00:01:36.897 --> 00:01:37.883
Four bytes a slot.

49
00:01:38.211 --> 00:01:40.400
One for the guitar onset, one for its

50
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.754
velocity, kept as the raw MIDI number so

51
00:01:42.754 --> 00:01:45.107
nothing is lost in rounding, and two for

52
00:01:45.107 --> 00:01:47.953
the drums: nine bits, one per drum, packed

53
00:01:47.953 --> 00:01:48.829
into one number.

54
00:01:49.306 --> 00:01:51.813
Twelve bytes a window: its tempo and its

55
00:01:51.813 --> 00:01:52.412
bar number.

56
00:01:52.739 --> 00:01:54.973
It stores the raw arrays, not the five

57
00:01:54.973 --> 00:01:57.371
inputs from lesson one point two, because

58
00:01:57.371 --> 00:01:59.551
the shift and the intensity's half-life

59
00:01:59.551 --> 00:02:01.458
are settings that experiments vary.

60
00:02:01.785 --> 00:02:04.019
Rebuilding the inputs takes microseconds.

61
00:02:04.481 --> 00:02:06.757
For Lakh v3, that's eighty-nine million

62
00:02:06.757 --> 00:02:08.870
slots and under four hundred megabytes.

63
00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:11.525
And the cache carries a fingerprint of the

64
00:02:11.525 --> 00:02:11.850
store.

65
00:02:12.175 --> 00:02:14.234
If the store has changed since it was

66
00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:16.672
built, the cache refuses to load, rather

67
00:02:16.672 --> 00:02:18.569
than serve old data without anyone

68
00:02:18.569 --> 00:02:19.056
noticing.

69
00:02:22.533 --> 00:02:25.085
The loader starts from a small table: each

70
00:02:25.085 --> 00:02:27.051
corpus names its store and its split.

71
00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:29.124
Lakh's two versions, and XMIDI.

72
00:02:29.585 --> 00:02:32.237
Every song sits on one side of the split:

73
00:02:32.237 --> 00:02:33.818
train, validation or test.

74
00:02:34.156 --> 00:02:36.640
Asking for the test side raises an error,

75
00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.897
unless the loader was built with a flag

76
00:02:38.897 --> 00:02:40.252
that says so on purpose.

77
00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.509
Spending the test set has to be a

78
00:02:42.509 --> 00:02:43.356
deliberate act.

79
00:02:44.027 --> 00:02:46.646
A song list, like XMIDI's rock songs, can

80
00:02:46.646 --> 00:02:47.443
narrow a side.

81
00:02:47.784 --> 00:02:50.175
Every song on the list must already be on

82
00:02:50.175 --> 00:02:52.737
that side, or the loader refuses, naming

83
00:02:52.737 --> 00:02:53.306
the songs.

84
00:02:53.648 --> 00:02:55.754
The genre lists are lesson two point

85
00:02:55.754 --> 00:02:56.096
seven.

86
00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:01.798
First, a tempo for every window.

87
00:03:02.118 --> 00:03:04.524
Two bars of four four are eight beats, and

88
00:03:04.524 --> 00:03:05.914
a minute is sixty seconds.

89
00:03:06.235 --> 00:03:08.373
Divide by the time between one window's

90
00:03:08.373 --> 00:03:09.389
start and the next.

91
00:03:09.839 --> 00:03:12.051
In this real Lakh song every window starts

92
00:03:12.051 --> 00:03:14.468
four seconds after the last: four hundred

93
00:03:14.468 --> 00:03:16.783
and eighty over four, a hundred and twenty

94
00:03:16.783 --> 00:03:17.657
beats per minute.

95
00:03:17.966 --> 00:03:19.355
So its tempo input is zero.

96
00:03:19.663 --> 00:03:21.875
Where two windows aren't neighbours, the

97
00:03:21.875 --> 00:03:23.830
gap is filled from the nearest window.

98
00:03:24.271 --> 00:03:26.617
A song with fewer than two windows has no

99
00:03:26.617 --> 00:03:28.962
tempo, and neither does one whose tempo

100
00:03:28.962 --> 00:03:30.358
map gives nothing usable.

101
00:03:30.693 --> 00:03:31.698
Those are dropped.

102
00:03:32.033 --> 00:03:34.602
In practice that's none: in both corpora

103
00:03:34.602 --> 00:03:36.556
the shortest song has four windows.

104
00:03:38.124 --> 00:03:40.079
Now the song's windows are laid end to

105
00:03:40.079 --> 00:03:40.280
end.

106
00:03:40.581 --> 00:03:42.737
x has one row per slot and five columns,

107
00:03:42.737 --> 00:03:44.742
the five inputs of lesson one point two.

108
00:03:45.043 --> 00:03:47.349
y has one row per slot and nine columns:

109
00:03:47.349 --> 00:03:49.355
the drums, which is H from lesson two

110
00:03:49.355 --> 00:03:50.508
point three, stacked.

111
00:03:50.809 --> 00:03:53.165
T, the number of rows, is thirty-two times

112
00:03:53.165 --> 00:03:55.672
the number of windows: here, seven hundred

113
00:03:55.672 --> 00:03:56.123
and four.

114
00:03:56.597 --> 00:03:58.691
The guitar's first notes come in slot

115
00:03:58.691 --> 00:03:58.966
five.

116
00:03:59.297 --> 00:04:01.996
x shows them one slot later, in slot six:

117
00:04:01.996 --> 00:04:04.255
the one-slot shift, so the model never

118
00:04:04.255 --> 00:04:05.853
hears the slot it's deciding.

119
00:04:06.183 --> 00:04:08.552
Meanwhile y has the drummer's kick in slot

120
00:04:08.552 --> 00:04:11.141
five: that's what the model must learn to

121
00:04:11.141 --> 00:04:11.417
play.

122
00:04:14.897 --> 00:04:16.760
Training doesn't take one song at a time.

123
00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:19.078
The training songs are shuffled, taken two

124
00:04:19.078 --> 00:04:20.850
hundred and fifty-six at a time, and

125
00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:22.895
sorted by length, then cut into batches of

126
00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:24.486
thirty-two songs of similar length.

127
00:04:24.759 --> 00:04:26.940
Here's a real one: the very first batch of

128
00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:28.167
the T one twenty-eight run.

129
00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.485
Thirty-two songs, from a hundred and sixty

130
00:04:30.485 --> 00:04:32.485
to nine hundred and ninety-two slots, one

131
00:04:32.485 --> 00:04:32.894
row each.

132
00:04:33.285 --> 00:04:35.468
The batch is walked forward in chunks of

133
00:04:35.468 --> 00:04:37.810
five hundred and twelve slots: sixteen

134
00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:38.236
windows.

135
00:04:38.556 --> 00:04:40.579
Each chunk is one training step:

136
00:04:40.579 --> 00:04:42.549
thirty-two songs by five hundred and

137
00:04:42.549 --> 00:04:44.625
twelve slots, sixteen thousand three

138
00:04:44.625 --> 00:04:46.595
hundred and eighty-four slots a step.

139
00:04:47.025 --> 00:04:48.791
Songs end at different places.

140
00:04:49.144 --> 00:04:51.735
Past its end, a song's row is padded with

141
00:04:51.735 --> 00:04:54.208
zeros, and a mask marks which slots are

142
00:04:54.208 --> 00:04:56.740
real: one for a real slot, zero for

143
00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:57.211
padding.

144
00:04:57.723 --> 00:05:00.089
So the loss is summed over the real slots

145
00:05:00.089 --> 00:05:02.286
only and divided by how many there are.

146
00:05:02.624 --> 00:05:03.977
Padding teaches nothing.

147
00:05:04.315 --> 00:05:06.681
How the loss itself works is lesson three

148
00:05:06.681 --> 00:05:07.245
point two.

149
00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:09.954
In this batch's first chunk, fourteen

150
00:05:09.954 --> 00:05:12.090
thousand eight hundred and forty-eight of

151
00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:14.074
the sixteen thousand three hundred and

152
00:05:14.074 --> 00:05:16.312
eighty-four slots are real: ninety-one

153
00:05:16.312 --> 00:05:16.719
percent.

154
00:05:17.024 --> 00:05:19.058
In the second, only six thousand nine

155
00:05:19.058 --> 00:05:20.279
hundred and seventy-six.

156
00:05:20.584 --> 00:05:22.822
Eight songs ended inside the first chunk,

157
00:05:22.822 --> 00:05:24.958
so their rows in the second chunk are all

158
00:05:24.958 --> 00:05:25.365
padding.

159
00:05:28.813 --> 00:05:30.743
A song longer than one chunk is cut, but

160
00:05:30.743 --> 00:05:31.461
its music isn't.

161
00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:33.525
At a batch's first chunk, the model's

162
00:05:33.525 --> 00:05:34.244
memory is reset.

163
00:05:34.513 --> 00:05:36.308
At every later chunk it carries on from

164
00:05:36.308 --> 00:05:38.327
where the last chunk stopped, so the model

165
00:05:38.327 --> 00:05:39.674
hears the whole song in order.

166
00:05:39.943 --> 00:05:41.828
But learning only looks back to the chunk

167
00:05:41.828 --> 00:05:43.488
edge: the gradient is cut there.

168
00:05:43.758 --> 00:05:45.777
That's truncated backpropagation, and it's

169
00:05:45.777 --> 00:05:46.854
lesson three point four.

170
00:05:50.277 --> 00:05:52.176
Here are the runs this machinery fed.

171
00:05:52.484 --> 00:05:54.692
The T one twenty-eight transformer trained

172
00:05:54.692 --> 00:05:56.540
on a fixed thirty percent of Lakh's

173
00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:58.696
training side: fourteen thousand two

174
00:05:58.696 --> 00:06:00.750
hundred and fifty-six songs, the same

175
00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:02.701
songs every epoch, two thousand one

176
00:06:02.701 --> 00:06:04.343
hundred and five steps an epoch.

177
00:06:04.651 --> 00:06:06.602
The genre drummers trained on XMIDI's

178
00:06:06.602 --> 00:06:09.067
lists: Rock, eight thousand five hundred

179
00:06:09.067 --> 00:06:11.171
and sixty-one songs and one thousand and

180
00:06:11.171 --> 00:06:13.584
sixteen steps; Jazz, five thousand five

181
00:06:13.584 --> 00:06:15.587
hundred and ninety-eight songs and six

182
00:06:15.587 --> 00:06:18.051
hundred and twenty-eight steps; Country,

183
00:06:18.051 --> 00:06:19.796
eleven thousand three hundred and

184
00:06:19.796 --> 00:06:21.901
sixty-eight songs and one thousand three

185
00:06:21.901 --> 00:06:23.339
hundred and seventeen steps.

186
00:06:23.764 --> 00:06:25.663
Fill is the share of a step's slots that

187
00:06:25.663 --> 00:06:27.886
are real: seventy percent on Lakh, about

188
00:06:27.886 --> 00:06:29.599
seventy-six to seventy-nine on XMIDI.

189
00:06:29.877 --> 00:06:31.729
Validation loss uses up to four hundred

190
00:06:31.729 --> 00:06:33.582
validation songs, walked the same way.

191
00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:35.620
A few architecture runs walked longer

192
00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:37.565
stretches with the same budget of sixteen

193
00:06:37.565 --> 00:06:39.371
thousand three hundred and eighty-four

194
00:06:39.371 --> 00:06:41.455
slots a step; that's horizon mode, in

195
00:06:41.455 --> 00:06:42.566
lesson three point four.

196
00:06:44.165 --> 00:06:46.873
Store, cache, loader: one song becomes x

197
00:06:46.873 --> 00:06:49.365
and y, thirty-two songs make a batch, and

198
00:06:49.365 --> 00:06:51.640
five hundred and twelve slots make a step.

199
00:06:51.965 --> 00:06:54.619
Next, in topic three: what the model does

200
00:06:54.619 --> 00:06:55.160
with them.

